    summary: 'A system call is the only sanctioned way for a user process to ask the Linux kernel to do something on its behalf: open a file, map memory, send on a socket, talk to a device driver. The CPU switches from user mode to kernel mode, the kernel validates arguments, does the work, and returns. On Linux the trap itself costs on the order of hundreds of nanoseconds, more with mitigations, so anything on a hot path avoids it.',
    purpose: 'Isolates the hardware and other processes from user code while giving it controlled access to files, memory, devices and the network.',
    mechanics: 'On x86-64 the process loads the syscall number and up to six arguments into registers and executes `syscall`; the CPU switches to the kernel stack, the handler dispatches through the syscall table, copies arguments and results across the boundary with `copy_from_user`/`copy_to_user`, and returns with `sysret`. The bare round trip is commonly a few hundred nanoseconds; Spectre/Meltdown mitigations such as KPTI (page-table isolation) add a CR3 write and TLB cost, so measured cost ranges from roughly 100 ns to a few microseconds depending on CPU and configuration. Read-only calls like `clock_gettime` and `gettimeofday` are served by the vDSO, a kernel-provided page mapped into every process, and never trap. For an LLM-serving process the calls that actually cross the boundary are socket I/O (`read`, `write`, `sendmsg`, `epoll_wait`), file I/O and `mmap` during weight loading, `futex` when Python or C++ threads block, and `ioctl` on `/dev/nvidia*` used by the user-mode driver for allocation, mapping and synchronization objects. CUDA kernel launches do not take a syscall: the user-mode driver writes commands into memory-mapped push buffers and rings a doorbell through an MMIO mapping, so a launch is a few microseconds of user-space CPU time with no kernel entry. A blocked `cudaStreamSynchronize` may spin in user space or block in the driver via ioctl, depending on the sync flags. `strace -c` and `perf trace` count and time calls; a serving process whose GPU loop shows thousands of syscalls per step is usually blocking on futexes or logging synchronously.',
    input: 'Syscall number and register arguments from user space.',
