    keywords: ['NVMe', 'queue depth', 'submission queue', 'completion queue', 'O_DIRECT', 'IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL', 'fio', 'iostat', 'blktrace', 'GPUDirect Storage'],
    summary: 'Block I/O moves data between the page cache or user buffers and storage devices. The multi-queue block layer (blk-mq) maps requests to per-CPU hardware queues; an NVMe SSD exposes many deep submission/completion queue pairs. io_uring is the modern asynchronous interface: submission and completion rings shared with the kernel, batched syscalls, optional kernel-side polling, and support for direct I/O into registered (pinned) buffers.',
    purpose: 'Keeps storage devices saturated with enough concurrent requests while spending little CPU per I/O, so checkpoints and datasets stream at device speed.',
    mechanics: 'A read that misses the page cache becomes a `bio` in the block layer, is merged and split into requests, and is placed on a blk-mq hardware queue that maps to an NVMe submission queue; the device writes a completion into the paired completion queue and raises an MSI-X interrupt (or is polled). Throughput scales with outstanding requests: a single NVMe drive needs a queue depth of tens of large requests to reach its rated several GB/s (example: 7 GB/s class PCIe 4.0 drives), so synchronous `read` from one thread leaves it mostly idle. Classic `epoll` does not apply to regular files (they are always readable), and POSIX AIO/libaio only works asynchronously with `O_DIRECT`. io_uring provides a submission queue and a completion queue in memory shared between user space and the kernel; the application writes SQEs, calls `io_uring_enter` once for a batch (or not at all with `SQPOLL`, where a kernel thread polls the ring), and reaps CQEs without syscalls. Registered buffers (`IORING_REGISTER_BUFFERS`) are pinned once so each I/O skips the get_user_pages cost, and `O_DIRECT` reads land in them straight from the device. Checkpoint streaming loaders combine these: aligned direct reads with high queue depth into pinned host buffers, then DMA to the GPU, overlapping storage, PCIe and device copies. GPUDirect Storage (cuFile) goes further by having the NVMe or NIC DMA directly into GPU memory, bypassing host RAM; it requires driver and filesystem support and is generation- and vendor-dependent.',
    input: 'Read/write requests from the page cache or from user buffers (direct I/O).',
