    summary: 'The Linux network stack carries HTTP/gRPC request and response bytes through sockets, TCP, IP and the NIC driver, with `epoll` letting one thread watch thousands of connections. Each send or receive crosses the syscall boundary and traverses the stack, costing microseconds of CPU per packet batch. Traffic that needs tens of GB/s with single-digit-µs latency (NCCL across nodes, KV transfer for disaggregated serving) bypasses the kernel entirely with RDMA verbs.',
    purpose: 'Gives applications reliable, multiplexed communication over shared NICs; provides bypass paths where the stack itself is the bottleneck.',
    mechanics: 'A `write`/`sendmsg` on a TCP socket copies user bytes into `sk_buff`s in the socket send buffer, segments them (GSO/TSO lets the NIC split large segments, saving per-packet stack cost), and hands them to the NIC driver ring; on receive, the NIC raises an interrupt, NAPI polls the ring in a softirq, GRO merges segments, and data lands in the receive buffer where `epoll` reports readiness. Per-packet CPU cost through the full stack is commonly 1-5 µs each way, so a socket path is limited to a few Gb/s per core for small messages and tens of Gb/s with large segments and offloads. Multiple NIC queues with RSS spread flows across cores; queue interrupt affinity decides which cores pay softirq time. A streaming LLM response is many small writes: with `TCP_NODELAY` unset, Nagle delays small segments; with it set, each token can become a packet, so servers commonly buffer a few tokens or rely on SSE chunking, adding milliseconds of jitter either way. Kernel bypass hands the NIC to user space: RDMA verbs (InfiniBand or RoCE) let a user process post sends and receives to hardware queue pairs and have the NIC DMA into registered (pinned) memory, including GPU memory via GPUDirect RDMA, with ~2 µs latency and no CPU per byte; DPDK and AF_XDP provide raw packet access for custom stacks. NCCL uses sockets only as a fallback or for bootstrap; its data path across nodes is verbs. TLS termination, proxies and service meshes add another stack traversal per hop.',
