    keywords: ['/dev/nvidia0', '/dev/nvidiactl', '/dev/nvidia-uvm', 'ioctl', 'nvidia-smi', 'dmesg Xid', 'RM', 'push buffer', 'doorbell', 'MMIO'],
    summary: 'The NVIDIA kernel-mode driver (on Linux: `nvidia.ko` plus `nvidia-uvm.ko` for unified memory, with `nvidia-drm`/`nvidia-modeset` for display) initializes the device, manages its memory and page tables, maps registers and command buffers into user processes, and handles interrupts. User-mode libraries (libcuda, the driver API) talk to it through `ioctl` on `/dev/nvidiactl` and `/dev/nvidiaN`. The kernel driver is on the path for allocation, mapping and synchronization; it is not on the path of each kernel launch.',
    purpose: 'Owns the privileged parts of GPU control (physical memory, page tables, interrupts, resets) so unprivileged processes can share a device safely.',
    mechanics: 'At load, `nvidia.ko` probes each PCIe device, maps its BARs (BAR0 registers, BAR1 a window into device memory), enables MSI-X, and on recent generations (Turing+, with the open kernel modules by default on Hopper and later) boots the GPU System Processor (GSP) firmware that runs much of the resource manager on the GPU itself. A process opens `/dev/nvidiactl` and `/dev/nvidiaN`; libcuda then uses a series of `ioctl` calls, commonly, to create a context (a GPU address space and channels), allocate device memory, pin and map host memory for DMA, map a push buffer and a doorbell register into user space, and create synchronization objects (semaphores/events). After that, launches are user-space: libcuda writes GPU commands into the mapped push buffer and writes the doorbell via MMIO, and the GPU\'s command processor fetches and executes them. The driver re-enters the picture for interrupts (completion, fault, error), for blocking waits when the process sleeps on an event, for `cudaMalloc`-scale allocations that the caching allocator has not already reserved, and for UVM: `nvidia-uvm.ko` services GPU page faults on managed memory by migrating pages between host and device and updating both page tables, at a cost of tens of microseconds per fault batch. It also implements the peer-to-peer and GPUDirect RDMA hooks (`nvidia-peermem`) that let NICs and other GPUs DMA to device memory, and reports Xid errors to `dmesg`. `nvidia-persistenced` keeps the device initialized between processes so the first context creation does not pay device init. The open-source kernel modules (`open-gpu-kernel-modules`) are the default for Turing and later on recent driver releases; the user-mode libraries remain proprietary, and this may vary by driver branch.',
