    summary: 'On a multi-socket server each CPU has its own memory controllers and PCIe lanes; memory on the other socket is reachable but slower and narrower. Linux allocates a page on the node of the CPU that first touches it unless a policy says otherwise. GPUs and NICs hang off one socket, so the pinned buffers they DMA to, and the threads that drive them, should live on that socket.',
    purpose: 'Keeps memory traffic local to the socket that generates it and keeps device DMA off the inter-socket link.',
    mechanics: 'The default policy is local allocation: a page fault is served from the node of the faulting CPU (first touch), falling back to other nodes when it is full. `numactl --membind=N` sets `MPOL_BIND` (fail or reclaim rather than spill), `--preferred=N` sets a soft preference, `--interleave` spreads pages round-robin; `set_mempolicy`/`mbind` do the same per process or per range. Remote access crosses the inter-socket link (UPI, Infinity Fabric), with latency commonly 1.5-2x local (for example ~90 ns local vs ~140-150 ns remote on a 2-socket server, example) and bandwidth limited by the link rather than the remote DRAM channels. A GPU on socket 0 doing DMA to a pinned buffer on socket 1 sends the traffic across that link twice (once for the DMA, and before that for the CPU to write the buffer), and shares it with all other cross-socket traffic; the same applies to a NIC receiving into a remote buffer. `nvidia-smi topo -m` prints the CPU affinity and NUMA node of each GPU and the link type between GPUs and NICs (NODE, PHB, PIX, NV#). The practical recipe is per-GPU processes started with `numactl --cpunodebind=<gpu node> --membind=<gpu node>` (or cgroup cpusets) so threads, pinned buffers and the device share a socket. Automatic NUMA balancing (`kernel.numa_balancing`) periodically unmaps pages to sample access locality and migrates them; it helps unpinned workloads but adds faults and can move pinned-adjacent buffers, so it is often disabled on serving hosts. `zone_reclaim_mode` decides whether the kernel reclaims local cache before allocating remotely. Huge pages and hugetlbfs pools are also per node and must be reserved on the right one.',
